<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/17th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Treaty · Peace</p><h1 class="enc-title">Peace of Westphalia</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1648">1648</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thirty-years-war">Thirty Years' War</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Peace of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/westphalia">Westphalia</a> was the series of treaties signed in 1648 that ended the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thirty-years-war">Thirty Years' War</a>. It recognised the right of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-roman-empire">Holy Roman Empire</a>'s princes to conduct their own foreign policy and confirmed their power over religion in their territories. It gave the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/swedish-empire">Swedish Empire</a> territories in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, recognised <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> independence from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, and helped shape the modern idea of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">territorial sovereignty</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/peace-of-westphalia.jpg" alt="Peace of Westphalia" width="262" height="420" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Dietmar Rabich · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:M%C3%BCnster,_Historisches_Rathaus_--_2014_--_6855.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
